<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">West Jersey</h1><p class="st-meta">1674 CE – 1702 CE · capital Burlington</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Quaker colony on the Delaware River whose 1677 Concessions and Agreements set out some of the most liberal rights of any 17th-century government.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1674 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-berkeley-1st-baron-berkeley-of-stratton">Lord Berkeley</a> sold his half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-fenwick-quaker">John Fenwick</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-byllynge">Edward Byllynge</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> became a trustee for the debt-ridden Byllynge. Fenwick founded Salem in 1675, and in 1677 a shipload of Quakers founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burlington-new-jersey">Burlington</a>, which became the capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintipartite-deed">Quintipartite Deed</a> of 1676 separated West <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Jersey</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-jersey">East Jersey</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Concessions and Agreements of 1677, associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">Penn</a>, guaranteed freedom of conscience, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">trial by jury</a> and an elected assembly with wide powers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quaker</a> meetings shaped community life, and the colony served as a model for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-pennsylvania">Province of Pennsylvania</a>. Farmers along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware</a> raised grain and livestock. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-byllynge">Byllynge</a>'s death the physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-coxe">Daniel Coxe</a> bought the governing rights in 1687 and sold them to the West <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Jersey</a> Society in 1692.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Disputes between proprietors and settlers led to the surrender of government in 1702, reuniting West and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-jersey">East Jersey</a> as the royal <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-new-jersey">Province of New Jersey</a>. The Council of Proprietors of West <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Jersey</a> still meets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burlington-new-jersey">Burlington</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/west-jersey.jpg" alt="West Jersey" width="273" height="318"><figcaption>Image: © 2003 Matthew Trump · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wpdms_east_west_new_jersey.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1674 CE – 1702 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Burlington</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edward Byllynge, Daniel Coxe, Samuel Jennings</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Quakerism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Province of West Jersey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1688&amp;focus=west-jersey" aria-label="West Jersey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2854 741 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where West Jersey lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3174 997L3277 927M3174 997L3175 977"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3277 927h0M3175 977h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3174 997h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3174 997h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1688&amp;focus